{
  "dom_id": "step-q78887",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x+1}{4}},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-1$ and $y=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x+1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{x+1}{4} \\\\ 4{y}^{3} & =x+1 \\\\ x & =4{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4{y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}-y{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-2|=2.$",
